    faq: [
      { q: 'What is classical architecture?', a: 'It is the tradition of building that descends from the temples of ancient Greece and Rome — a shared language of columns, proportion and ornament, revived and argued over in every century since. It is not one style but a family of them, all speaking the same grammar.' },
      { q: 'What are the “three tests” of a building?', a: 'They come from Vitruvius: firmitas, that it stand soundly; utilitas, that it serve its use; and venustas, that it give delight to the eye. Rendered as firmness, commodity and delight, they are the fastest way to judge any building, ancient or modern — every part of the tradition is an attempt to satisfy all three at once.' },
      { q: 'How do I begin reading buildings on my own street?', a: 'Look at the top of the columns or pilasters — a plain cushion is Doric, scrolls are Ionic, leaves are Corinthian — and name the order aloud. Then find the centre and the two ends of the façade and see how the ornament gathers there, and trace what carries what down to the ground. Two minutes of really looking beats a page of reading.' },
      { q: 'Is “classical” only ancient, or is it still around today?', a: 'It is very much living. The tradition has died and been reborn many times — the Italian Renaissance reviving Rome, Palladio reviving the ancients, the Georgian and Greek Revivals, and the Beaux-Arts civic buildings of the modern age. The bank, the museum and the city hall down the road are living chapters of the same story.' },
    ],
    books: [
      { title: 'De Architectura (The Ten Books on Architecture)', author: 'Vitruvius, 1st c. BC', note: 'PRIMARY. The only architectural treatise to survive from antiquity, and the root of the whole tradition. Dry in places, but the source of everything. The Morgan translation is free online.' },
      { title: 'The Four Books of Architecture', author: 'Andrea Palladio, 1570', note: 'PRIMARY. Part manifesto, part pattern-book, gorgeously illustrated. The single most influential architecture book ever printed. Free scans exist.' },
      { title: 'The Seven Lamps of Architecture', author: 'John Ruskin, 1849', note: 'PRIMARY (modern). Not a how-to but a passionate argument for what building is for. Read the “Lamp of Memory” even if you read nothing else.' },
      { title: 'The Classical Language of Architecture', author: 'John Summerson, 1963', note: 'THE way in. Short, brilliant, and exactly the “language” metaphor this app is built on. Read it first.' },
      { title: 'A History of Architecture', author: 'Sir Banister Fletcher (any modern edition)', note: 'ON-RAMP. The great reference. Not for reading through, but unmatched for looking things up with clear comparative drawings.' },
    ],
